  "question": "Suppose $y$ is defined implicitly as $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 3$. Find $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at the point $(1,1)$.",
  "answeroption": "B",
  "explanation": "Differentiate:<br /><br />$2x+y+(x+2y)\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$.<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x+y}{x+2y}$.<br /><br />At $(1,1)$, $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-1$.<br /><br />Differentiating again and substituting $x=1$, $y=1$, $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-1$:<br /><br />$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=-\\frac{2}{3}$.",
